🔁 Self-Preservation Mode (Important for Interviews)
When Eureka server stops receiving heartbeats, it doesn't immediately remove services.

This protects against network partitions — called Self-Preservation Mode

Can be disabled in test environments with:

yaml
Copy
Edit
eureka:
  server:
    enable-self-preservation: false
📉 Eureka vs Consul vs Zookeeper
| Feature              | Eureka      | Consul      | Zookeeper   |
| -------------------- | ----------- | ----------- | ----------- |
| Developed By         | Netflix     | HashiCorp   | Apache      |
| Service Discovery    | ✅           | ✅           | ✅           |
| Health Check         | Client-side | Server-side | Server-side |
| Built-in UI          | ✅           | ✅           | ❌           |
| Spring Cloud Support | ✅           | ✅           | Partial     |


❓ Interview Questions & Answers
Q1: What is Eureka in Spring Cloud?
A: Eureka is a service discovery tool where microservices can register and discover each other without hardcoding network addresses.

Q2: How does Eureka handle failures?
A: Through self-preservation mode, Eureka avoids removing services from the registry during temporary network issues.

Q3: What's the difference between Eureka Server and Eureka Client?
A: Server holds the registry; clients register themselves and discover others.

Q4: What is the default port for Eureka Server?
A: 8761

Q5: Can Eureka work in a production-ready cluster?
A: Yes, Eureka servers can be set up in a peer-to-peer cluster (high availability).

